;; Why this leaks nodes (21 pairs, Redexes: 0, exit 1).
;;
;; The program computes correctly: (fix f) 1 unrolls twice -
;;   cond(identity 1, (self 0), 0) -> test forces to 1 -> intCond keeps
;;   (self 0); cond(identity 0, ...) -> test forces to 0 -> intCond
;;   returns 0. The result 0 is discarded.
;;
;; The leak is structural, not a bug in this test:
;;
;; 1. fix in hvm-core.toc is (defn fix [f] (f (fix f))) - self-recursion
;;    through its own global REF. f is used twice in the body, so codegen
;;    emits dupeArg(f_1, &f_1_1, 9). f is a LAM (non-native), so
;;    makeLazyDup builds the LAZ/DUP self-referential sharing network
;;    (the "D 9" / "S 9" nodes in the graphs, the "SUP 9" at 0x46 in the
;;    final buffer dump).
;;
;; 2. intCond (runtime3.c:3170) selects a branch with
;;    interact(ERA, droppedBranch) + move(portLoc(2, args), keptBranch).
;;    The kept branch is re-inserted as a VAR indirection into a thunk
;;    that is never re-forced through the same path, and the DUP/lazy-LAZ
;;    network that correlated the two uses of f is consumed only along
;;    the path that actually ran.
;;
;; 3. The machine has no garbage collector: HVM pairs are freed only when
;;    an interaction touches them. The unforced half of the network (the
;;    APP/LAZ/VAR web at 0x0a-0x46, incl. the self-cycle APP@0x40 <->
;;    LAZ@0x0c) is orphaned but never ERA'd. take on LAZ/SUB deliberately
;;    leaves the location allocated (implementation.md), so those thunks
;;    persist. Each terminated fix recursion leaves its unforced thunk
;;    web behind.
;;
;; Same known pattern as the reader's fix-based many
;; (interpreter/intrp-rdr.toc: "TODO: this version leaks nodes").
;;

;; This programs leaks nodes because of the way 'fix' is written

;; (defn fix [f]
;;   (f (fix f)))

(defn main [argv]
  (((fn [p]
      (fix (fn [self]
             (fn [s]
               (cond (p s)
                     (self 0)
                     0)))))
    identity)
   1))  
